Hi Gabriele, please find my replies below in red:


* are we sharing information on all drivers or only active ones? What is the specific requirement? - {color:#ff5630}This would be for active drivers, and the requirement is coming out of the DAC7 reporting regulations where we (Bolt) as a platform operator are required to report to the relevant tax authorities under the EU DAC7 rules, which oblige platform operators like us to provide details, inter alia, about seller identification, their income and activities conducted through the platform. This measure is part of an EU-wide effort to enhance tax transparency and compliance across all EU Member States.{color}

* Is this requirement coming from the law (if so, how does it sound?), or a licensing agreement/licensing conditions? - {color:#ff5630}As per the above legislation, we are required to share relevant data with the tax authorities under EU DAC7 regulations, and therefore each partner on the rides vertical is required to provided their Tax Identification Number and will be blocked from operating on the platform until this is validated / verified. As per my conversation with the tax authorities yesterday, this is different to validate as the format of the TIN number for foreign self-employed partners may vary from person to person (i.e. it may not always be a 9-digit number) and they offered to validate via email.{color}
* Is this applicable to drivers only, or also couriers? - {color:#ff5630}From our end, we are only looking at drivers under the rides vertical.{color}
* Can we choose which number to provide (ID card number or residence permit number), or will we have to provide both? - {color:#ff5630}We can choose to only provide ID card number.{color}
* How will we distinguish which partner is a foreigner? - {color:#ff5630}All foreigners residing in Malta will have a residence permit and ID card number ending in “A”{color}

