Hi [~accountid:712020:7f47c2af-14e1-4206-a795-ac87cb906276] [~accountid:712020:c0f6fbe7-f5ae-44e2-bb6c-8e5815ace0d9] , reviewed the DPA, flagging some clauses for your attention:

* 7.5. The Processor undertakes to continuously log access to the Personal Data under the PUB Agreement to the extent required by the Instruction. Logs may not be deleted until five (5) years after the time of logging, unless otherwise stated in the Instructions. Logs shall be subject to the necessary safeguards, in accordance with Data Protection Legislation. - {color:#4c9aff}this needs to be flagged to Infosec team - I am not sure if we have the technical ability to log all access to personal data throughout different systems and databases (but maybe we do). Suggest checking with Tarmo Randel.{color}
* 8.3. The Processor shall promptly inform the Controller of any contacts with supervisory authorities regarding the Processing. The Processor shall not be authorised to represent the Controller or act on behalf of the Controller in relation to supervisory authorities in matters relating to the Processing - {color:#4c9aff}this is problematic due to the controller<>processor setup that’s set in this agreement. We’ll be able to inform them about a regulatory investigation if it directly affects the riders whose data we processed as part of this specific tender, but not much more. We constantly receive regulatory investigations in the EU concerning both riders and drivers. Unless these investigations/inquiries from data protection authorities will be directly Swedish rider complaints, we won’t be able to inform them. Not a redflag, standard clause but hard to implement in practice. {color}
* 8.4. If the Data Subject, supervisory authority or third party requests information from the Processor which relates to the Processing, the Processor shall inform the Controller thereof. Information about the Processing shall not be disclosed to the Data Subject, supervisory authority or third party without the written consent of the Controller, unless mandatory law requires disclosure. The Processor shall assist in conveying the information covered by a consent or legal requirement - {color:#4c9aff}we do receive GDPR requests from users, as well as police requests, and we must comply with those. We’ll need to find a way to monitor whether user X is a user who received services under this setup. Otherwise, we might objectively unable to comply with this clause. Not a redflag, standard clause but hard to implement in practice. {color}
* *Data breaches - general comment -* we do suffer data breaches from time to time, especially due to reasons we cannot control, such as phishing, account takeover, recycled phone number. In this case, we will be liable for data breaches and accountable towards Sigtuna. To mitigate this, would it be possible to ask all riders to undergo MFA/verify their email address for the use of our service? I understand it’s not the most pleasant solution from an operational POV, but I suggest thinking about this to minimize the potential risk.
* *Setup and agreements with Fleets/Drivers* - We will be acting as a data processor and processing data under instructions of Sigtuna. This means that drivers and fleets will be considered as pre-approved sub-processors. Now, just to be clear - we are required to conclude data processing agreements with those fleets and/or drivers. Are we preparing any custom T&Cs, or are we relying on our current T&Cs that are published online? Will we be contracting with fleets or with drivers?
** I don’t know how this will work in practice, but we are obligated to inform Sigtuna in case we share data with a new fleet/driver. 
** Related to the above is clause 12.8 “_The Processor shall at all times keep an accurate and up-to-date list of the Sub-Processors engaged to Process Personal Data on behalf of the Controller and make this list available to the Controller. The list shall in particular specify the country in which the Sub-processor processes the Personal Data and the types of Processing carried out by the Sub-processor._ “ - we’ll need to keep a list of fleets/drivers working with this tender.
* *Data localization requirement 13.1* “The Processor shall ensure that the Personal Data is processed and stored within the EU/EEA by a natural or legal person established in the EU/EEA, unless the parties to the PUB Agreement agree otherwise. “ - {color:#4c9aff}while we do keep most data in the EU, it is impossible to avoid data transfers outside EU, especially for marketing, where data goes to the US/Israel etc. But we don’t have a full list of data recipients outside EU/EEA, it’d take us a long time to map it. Perhaps we can push for a clause that authorizes us to transfer data outside EEA/EU if we ensure that we apply safeguards that are mandatory under law? We always follow data transfer rules approved the European Commission (such as Standard Contractual Clauses) when we transfer data.{color}
* *Data deletion -* under Section 17, upon termination, we will need to delete all data. This is not possible as we are required to retain certain data under law for tax and other purposes.
